ssional districts here in  nevada and the idea is to get an equal  number of people in each district based  on the census so you can see here for  example the population deviation from  ideal this mark here is congresswoman  susie lee's district and this  is congresswoman titus as you can see  the difference there in the populations  the bottom line is redistricting will  sort of even those numbers out every 10  years the census and the legislature  sort out the boundaries of the  congressional legislative districts to  try and make sure it's even and fair  but it's just like a bill both houses  have to pass it then the governor signs  it we spoke with unlv political science  assistant professor dan lee about this  process  how much is nevada going to change from  an electoral process from a government  process from a representative process  10 years on how different does nevada  look right now so i think in terms of  representation the big question in terms  of redistricting is out of the four  seats how many are going to be  represented by democrats versus  republicans right because on the one  hand you look at nevada where swing  state you know the statewide elections  are all pretty competitive it's close to  50 50 split also just looking at party  registration of voters so you might  expect well if it's 50 50 then democrats  you get two republicans should get two  that seems like the most fair option or  most fair outcome  right but this is where  you know people start talking about  gerrymandering and you know kind of the  dirty side of redistricting um because  democrats are you know they currently  have a three to one advantage and  they're going to try to uh shore up that  advantage so they're going to try to  redraw the districts  that uh probably makes the third  district for instance a little bit safer  for susie lee republican in the state  legislature they're going to fight  against that redistricting plan  right and they're going to try to keep  it closer to a 202 split right or at  least keep it where some of these  districts such as the third district are  still in play for  republicans how do you envision her just  based on  what we have so right now  will how will the districts be  redrawn what's that battle going to be  like one way we can think about this  it's just an analogy where we need to  make a jigsaw puzzle out of nevada right  that's essentially what redistricting is  we have the picture of nevada and we're  just trying to cut it up into uh some  jigsaw pieces so we got to cut it up  into four pieces  right so  so there are constraints right it's not  where democrats republicans just can  just do whatever they want  right so for instance um  you know these jigsaw each jigsaw piece  is one congressional district so you  can't have like two separate jigsaw  pieces being represented by one  representative by one person right  so they have to be contiguous  another thing is they have to be compact  they can't be too big and sprawly and  weird shape although you do see weird  shape districts yeah that's like one of  the constraints right and you need to  respect um communities of interest so by  that you don't really want to split up  counties or cities like where you can  you try to keep people uh that have a  similar economic and political interest  together in the same congressional  district right so with those constraints  we can kind of see you know look ahead  of you know what's going to happen in  this next round of redistricting where a  lot of it's going to look the same it's  just shading enough to  secure what is already in place versus  gaining anything i don't think there's  any expectation by the democrats of  trying to take uh  mark emmett  right exactly right because again there  are constraints right again  there's limits to what the jigsaw pieces  can look like so again that's going to  leave the northern you know amadeus seat  you know relatively safer for him right  and  yes the yo